afds-sample: close E1 in the evidence matrix

E1 asks that the matrix include speech recognition and Reflow
environment details: device, browser, starting viewport, and zoom.
The sample met none of that, and the website now says so publicly, so
the choice was to leave the gap documented or close it. Closing it.

Record shape. Three fields added, taking a record from fifteen to
eighteen. `browser` was already top level, so nesting a
reflowEnvironment object containing it again would have put one fact
in two places, which is what the ownership rule in clause 6.2 exists
to prevent. Flat additions keep the matrix tabular and every record
the same shape.

`device` is universal, because platform and device are not the same
axis. Windows is not desktop, and a screen reader on a phone is a
different observation from the same screen reader on a desktop.
`startingViewport` and `zoom` apply to reflow claims and read
not-applicable elsewhere.

Two senses of not-applicable. The file defined it only as a result,
meaning the combination cannot exhibit the behaviour, yet already
used it as a field value on atVersion without saying so. Three more
fields would have made an undocumented convention load-bearing, so
the description now separates the field sense from the result sense.

Four new records. Two reflow records hold engine, browser, platform
and device constant so that startingViewport and zoom are the only
fields that differ, the same device the NVDA and JAWS records use to
show engine and assistive technology as independent axes. One sits at
a 320 CSS pixel viewport, the other at 1280 by 1024 with 400% zoom.

Two speech-recognition records. A cheaper option was one record
reading result not-applicable, which I rejected: the vocabulary
scopes not-applicable to the platform lacking a feature, not the
component, and it would teach that speech recognition is irrelevant
to layout. Instead the claim is non-interference, that the container
introduces no element receiving pointer or voice-driven activation,
which is the same shape as every other Stack guarantee. A layout
primitive's guarantees are non-interference guarantees. Product names
are as their vendors write them: Microsoft writes voice access in
lower case, Apple writes Voice Control.

Uncertainty grows from two entries to four. Reflow records needed
something to reference, and the reflow claims lived only as manual
assertions stack-a4 and stack-a5. Under the propagation rule a manual
assertion with no recorded observation is uncertainty, so the sample
was asserting both while holding no evidence for either. stack-u3
makes that explicit for a4 and stack-u4 covers the voice claim.

That leaves stack-a5 and stack-a6 as manual assertions with no
uncertainty entry and no matrix row, so the propagation rule still
has not been applied to them. known-limitations.md now records that
rather than leaving it implied.

Also corrected there: the file said every version field carries the
placeholder, which was never true. The record with no assistive
technology carries not-applicable for atVersion. README.md repeated
the same error and is fixed too.

Every new result is not-yet-tested. Nothing in this commit claims an
observation took place.

Inventory rebuilt with tools/build-inventory.py. Three digests
change, for the two files edited inside the package plus stack.md.
Verify passes on nine entries and the tree packs.

Not settled and deliberately unchanged: packageVersion and the
component version stay at 1.0.0 while the package is a draft, though
adding uncertainty entries arguably touches the component contract.
afdsEvidenceVersion goes to 1.1.0, which is this file's own
convention rather than anything the format document defines.
